{"name":"Flower Pot","hex":"#8f4438","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"flower pot","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Alternative form of flowerpot."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/flower_pot"}],"entries":[{"title":"Flowerpot","blocks":[{"text":"A flowerpot, planter, planterette or plant pot is a container in which flowers and other plants are cultivated and displayed. Historically, and still to a significant extent today, they are made from plain terracotta with no ceramic glaze, with a round shape, tapering inwards towards the bottom. Flowerpots are now often also made from plastic, metal, wood, stone, or sometimes biodegradable material. An example of biodegradable pots are ones made of heavy brown paper, cardboard, or peat moss in which young plants for transplanting are grown."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9a/%22Meillandine%22_Rose_in_clay_pot.jpg/330px-%22Meillandine%22_Rose_in_clay_pot.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flowerpot"},{"title":"Flower","blocks":[{"text":"Flowers, also known as blossoms and blooms, are the reproductive structures of flowering plants. Typically, they are structured in four circular levels around the end of a stalk. These include: sepals, which are modified leaves that support the flower; petals, often designed to attract pollinators; male stamens, where pollen is presented; and female gynoecia, where pollen is received and its movement is facilitated to the egg. When flowers are arranged in a group, they are known collectively as an inflorescence."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/Magnolia_grandiflora_-_flower_1.jpg/330px-Magnolia_grandiflora_-_flower_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flower"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cd/Shelves_of_flower_pots_in_Darwin%27s_laboratory%2C_Down_House_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1200541.jpg/500px-Shelves_of_flower_pots_in_Darwin%27s_laboratory%2C_Down_House_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1200541.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cd/Shelves_of_flower_pots_in_Darwin%27s_laboratory%2C_Down_House_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1200541.jpg/500px-Shelves_of_flower_pots_in_Darwin%27s_laboratory%2C_Down_House_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1200541.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Traditional flowerpots in unglazed terracotta in Charles Darwin's laboratory at Down House","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AShelves_of_flower_pots_in_Darwin's_laboratory%2C_Down_House_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1200541.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/99/Palazzo_socci%2C_terrazza%2C_vaso_in_terracotta_con_festoni.jpg/500px-Palazzo_socci%2C_terrazza%2C_vaso_in_terracotta_con_festoni.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/99/Palazzo_socci%2C_terrazza%2C_vaso_in_terracotta_con_festoni.jpg/1280px-Palazzo_socci%2C_terrazza%2C_vaso_in_terracotta_con_festoni.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/99/Palazzo_socci%2C_terrazza%2C_vaso_in_terracotta_con_festoni.jpg/500px-Palazzo_socci%2C_terrazza%2C_vaso_in_terracotta_con_festoni.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Terracotta flowerpot in Italy, decorated with festoons","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APalazzo_socci%2C_terrazza%2C_vaso_in_terracotta_con_festoni.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/17/JiffyPots.JPG/500px-JiffyPots.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbn